WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.068
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.983
<v Examiner>Describe the chain of infection,

3
00:00:03.983 --> 00:00:08.111
<v Examiner>and explain how standard and transmission-based precautions break it,

4
00:00:08.111 --> 00:00:11.821
<v Examiner>with an example of each type of transmission-based precaution.

5
00:00:12.271 --> 00:00:14.724
The chain of infection has six links.

6
00:00:14.724 --> 00:00:17.841
The infectious agent: bacteria, viruses, fungi.

7
00:00:17.841 --> 00:00:23.080
The reservoir, where it survives: people, water, food, soil, animals, surfaces.

8
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.318
The portal of exit, how it leaves: the respiratory tract, blood, skin, the gut.

9
00:00:28.318 --> 00:00:32.562
The mode of transmission: contact, droplet, airborne, ingestion.

10
00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:37.469
The portal of entry: broken skin, mucous membranes, the respiratory tract.

11
00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:43.172
And the susceptible host: babies, older people, pregnant women, the immunocompromised,

12
00:00:43.172 --> 00:00:45.759
patients in hospital, the unvaccinated.

13
00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:48.610
Break any one link and the infection stops.

14
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.371
Standard precautions are the minimum for every patient, whatever their known status,

15
00:00:54.371 --> 00:00:57.279
whenever you might touch blood or body fluids,

16
00:00:57.279 --> 00:01:00.566
because you can't tell by looking who is infectious.

17
00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:05.118
They are hand hygiene, which is the most effective single method; P P E;

18
00:01:05.118 --> 00:01:09.291
respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette; safe sharps, never recap;

19
00:01:09.291 --> 00:01:13.084
linen and waste management; aseptic technique; and cleaning.

20
00:01:13.084 --> 00:01:16.055
They break the chain at transmission and entry.

21
00:01:16.505 --> 00:01:22.449
Transmission-based precautions are added on top, depending on how the organism spreads.

22
00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:27.231
Contact precautions: gown and gloves for every encounter, for M R S A,

23
00:01:27.231 --> 00:01:30.988
E S B L and gastro bugs, like a patient with diarrhoea.

24
00:01:30.988 --> 00:01:36.726
Droplet precautions: a medical mask, eye protection, gown and gloves, for influenza,

25
00:01:36.726 --> 00:01:38.161
mumps and meningitis.

26
00:01:38.161 --> 00:01:43.831
Airborne precautions: an N ninety-five respirator, eye protection, gown and gloves,

27
00:01:43.831 --> 00:01:48.545
and a negative-pressure room, for measles, chickenpox, T B and COVID.

28
00:01:48.545 --> 00:01:53.122
So a child with blisters and suspected chickenpox goes on airborne.

29
00:01:53.572 --> 00:01:58.295
Healthcare-associated infections are the most frequently reported adverse event in

30
00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:00.484
healthcare, which is why this matters.

31
00:02:00.934 --> 00:02:06.356
Chain of infection: agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission,

32
00:02:06.356 --> 00:02:08.814
portal of entry, susceptible host.

33
00:02:08.814 --> 00:02:15.031
Standard precautions for everyone: hand hygiene first, P P E, cough etiquette, sharps,

34
00:02:15.031 --> 00:02:16.838
waste, aseptic technique.

35
00:02:16.838 --> 00:02:20.091
Contact: gown and gloves, M R S A and gastro.

36
00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:21.898
Droplet: mask, influenza.

37
00:02:21.898 --> 00:02:27.609
Airborne: N ninety-five and a negative-pressure room, measles, chickenpox, T B.
